<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ngasaunggyan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1277">1277</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mongol-invasion-of-burma">First Mongol invasion of Burma</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ngasaunggyan was fought in 1277 near the frontier between the Pagan Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> refused the tribute demanded by the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. Yuan cavalry from Yunnan defeated a Burmese army that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, a battle described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>. It was the first engagement of the Mongol invasions that led to the collapse of Pagan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> sent emissaries to the powers of eastern Asia to demand tribute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> of Pagan turned away the Khan's representatives on their first visit in 1271. On a later tribute mission, in 1273, the Mongol envoys were killed by bandits, and Kublai Khan did not respond at once. In 1277 Narathihapate invaded the state of Kaungai, whose chief had recently pledged fealty to Kublai Khan, and the local garrisons of Yuan troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> were ordered to defend it.</p><p>There is no evidence that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> led his army himself. The Burmese chronicles regard him as incompetent and cowardly and make no clear mention of the battle; if he was present, command probably lay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a> or another general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Burmese advanced with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and the ponies of the Yuan horse archers became uncontrollable, nearly causing a rout. The Yuan general Khudu (Qutuq) restored order by having his men dismount, tether their horses in a nearby wood and fight on foot. Their arrows could not pierce the armoured elephants, but the volleys terrified them until they turned and trampled their own troops.</p><p>The Yuan troops then remounted and charged the broken Burmese lines, first with arrows and then with sabres and maces. The Burmese infantry withstood several charges, and the Yuan took heavy losses, but their better armour let them wear down the unarmoured Burmese levies until the whole host fled. Khudu was wounded, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, having lost many men, did not pursue far and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> to regroup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At the end of 1277 Naser al-Din, son of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhamo">Bhamo</a> and tried to set up a postal system, but deadly heat forced him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>; in 1279 he brought 12 elephants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>. Ngasaunggyan was the first of three decisive battles between the two powers, followed by the Battle of Bhamo in 1283 and the Battle of Pagan in 1287. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>'s vivid description was presumably pieced together from accounts he heard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ngasaunggyan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pagan’s refusal led to the <b>Battle of Ngasaunggyan</b> in 1277, near the Yunnan border, where Mongol troops defeated a Burmese army with elephants, a battle described by Marco Polo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
